[# docs-src/repos/cx-decisions.cxd — the record's page,
   repo-cx-decisions.html (RULED: DOCS-41, DOCS-47, DOCS-49 wave 4). Facts
   from the registries; the repository has no corpus of its own, so the one
   example is a case of the language corpus whose own notes name the ledger
   page that ruled it — the substitution RESULTS.md names. #]

[repo name=cx-decisions
  [intro
    """A decision is written down once, and a commit that cites it
       has to find it. `cx-decisions` is the record: the ledger of every
       ruling under its id, the process specs that give the work its
       vocabulary — release, epic, issue, decision; branch, merge; owner,
       integrator, agent; step, pipeline, run — the standing rules every
       agent reads before its brief, and the design texts a ruling was
       argued from."""]
  [body
    """A `RULED: <id>` in a commit subject is not decoration: the freeze gate
       resolves every id against the ledger and refuses a commit that names
       a decision the ledger does not carry, and the ledger's index maps
       every id to the file and heading that carries it — so a rule is
       either recorded or it is not a rule. The ledger records decisions; it
       does not make them. The spec is the only truth, and a decision that
       changes it is an edit to the spec inside the decision, never a
       document beside it."""]
  [body
    """What is here is what every other repository reads and none of them
       owns: the delivery grammar, governance, the readiness rubric and the
       release process; the pin-document format every `deps.cxd` follows;
       the standing rules; the working specs and the archived ones; the
       design texts and each release's own record. The tree this
       site was built from keeps the tracked copies its gates read, and this
       repository is the record published for the rest."""]
  [gives
    """No module and no code: the repository is text — the ledger, the
       process specs, the rules and the designs — so the table below is the
       generator's note. What it gives you is the reason behind every
       `RULED:` on this site."""]
  [try
    """A decision and its executable end, side by side. This case's own
       notes name the ledger page that ruled it: a monitor's batch receive
       answers the channel family's terminal when there will never be
       anything more — a drained monitor, a closed one — instead of an empty
       answer that could also mean not yet, and the corpus pins exactly that.
       The case is the language corpus's; the ruling it cites is a page of
       this repository's ledger."""]
  [fixture id=program-conc-028b-monitor-batch-terminal-answers]
  [status
    """The front door pins nothing of the record — its gates read their own
       tracked copies of the ledger and the process specs — so the registry
       row carries an `at=` and `deps.cxd` carries no row, and the figure
       draws the repository alone. Nothing builds on it in the build's
       sense; everything is ruled by it."""]]
